From 39348c9ce7215a17c6af35eadcfe06f63f6f3aba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Jonathan D.A. Jewell" <6759885+hyperpolymath@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 14:28:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Replace zeros only where the threshold can keep them
 small

Four asks from #25's review, in the order they bite.

`min_prevalence` now selects the composition as well as the taxa tested, and it
is applied before any replacement is computed: `clr_composition` keeps the taxa
that have reads and reach the threshold, their zeros are replaced, every sample
is transformed over exactly those taxa, and the threshold is not applied again
afterwards. On the sparse 18S table that prompted the review, replacing across
every observed taxon imputed a median 47% of each sample - over 100% of the
total in 9 of 66 samples, which is a second dataset rather than a replacement -
where filtering at 0.1 first leaves a median of 1%. The refusal of an over-full
sample is computed in Julia before R is called, and its advice is now
arithmetic the caller can act on: the largest delta the sample admits, and the
threshold that clears it, derived from the same numbers that raised it.

The statistics moved into pinned R rather than staying in Julia. The
replacement is `zCompositions::multRepl` at `frac = delta` with per-cell
detection limits, asked with `z.delete = FALSE` and `z.warning = 1` so the
package cannot silently drop the samples and taxa the result is about to
report; the test per taxon is Welch's `stats::t.test`, contrast minus
reference, with R's own statistic and p-value reconciled against a standard
error computed from the same two groups before either is returned.
zCompositions 1.6.2 and its imports NADA and truncnorm are pinned in
renv.lock, declared in `R/_renv_dependencies.R` and required in CI; a missing
package raises `ZCompositionsUnavailable` (503 from the route) rather than
falling back. The Julia formula remains as `mult_repl_reference`, labelled
`via = "julia reference"`, because the refusals have to know the imputed mass
before anything reaches R and because the equivalence of the two is now a test
rather than an assumption.

A `clr_lm` comparison also needs two samples in each group, since a t test
cannot estimate a variance from one; `nb_glm` is unchanged. The three lockfile
entries were written by hand and should be regenerated by `renv::snapshot()`
under R 4.5.0.

Tests: the reference formula and the guard on known tables; refusal text
checked against the advice it gives; parity of the R replacement with the
Julia reference and with `cmultRepl(method = "CZM")`, which it is not; the
Welch estimate as the difference of group mean CLRs and against a direct
`t.test` call; `clr_composition` semantics; and the sparse 40x24 fixture the
review asked for - about 95% zeros, refused at min_prevalence 0, running at
0.05 with the twenty singleton taxa filtered and nothing imputed.

Refs #25, #102
